WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.432
Let's summarize Module 3. SELECT defines the data you want to retrieve.

00:00:05.432 --> 00:00:09.547
WHERE and related predicates decide which rows qualify.

00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:12.262
ORDER BY, LIMIT, and DISTINCT help organize the result.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:16.232
INSERT adds rows. UPDATE changes rows.

00:00:16.232 --> 00:00:18.262
DELETE can remove rows.

00:00:18.262 --> 00:00:24.992
Because those commands change stored data, previewing targets and using stable keys are important safety

00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:27.982
habits. Constraints protect data rules and relationships.

00:00:27.982 --> 00:00:35.997
Transactions give you a boundary where changes can be committed or rolled back.

00:00:35.997 --> 00:00:39.507
RETURNING helps you inspect what PostgreSQL changed.

00:00:39.507 --> 00:00:45.312
JSONB extends PostgreSQL to semi-structured values, while INSERT INTO SELECT connects query results to

00:00:45.312 --> 00:00:51.972
data-preparation workflows. The habit to carry forward is simple: understand the request, preview the target,

00:00:51.972 --> 00:00:58.997
make the smallest correct change, inspect the result, and verify before you keep it.

00:00:58.997 --> 00:01:01.207
Do not treat this as a list to memorize.

00:01:01.207 --> 00:01:06.232
Try to explain each idea using one example from a project you understand.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:12.407
If you can describe when you would use the concept, what assumption it makes,

00:01:12.407 --> 00:01:18.230
and how you would validate the result, you are ready to apply it independently.
